<BaseLayout
  title="Winnebago County & Rockford Surveillance | NoRec.US"
  description="Documented surveillance technology used by Winnebago County and Rockford-area law-enforcement agencies, including Flock ALPR, Axon body cameras, drones, fixed cameras, gunshot detection, and social-media analytics."
  active="surveillance"
>
<main class="page_shell">
  <header class="article_header">
    <p class="eyebrow">LOCAL SURVEILLANCE · WINNEBAGO COUNTY / ROCKFORD, ILLINOIS</p>
    <h1>Winnebago County and Rockford</h1>
    <p class="article_deck">County and city records document overlapping ALPR networks, aerial surveillance, body-worn video, fixed cameras, and investigative software.</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">WINNEBAGO COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE</p>
    <h2>Countywide Flock deployment and drones</h2>
    <div class="finding_grid">
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Five-year Flock agreement</h3>
        <p>Winnebago County approved a five-year agreement with Flock Group in May 2024 for license-plate readers deployed throughout the county. The appropriation was $719,250, with $174,250 for the first year and $136,250 for each subsequent year.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Federal-seizure funding</h3>
        <p>The county's executive summary identifies the Federal Seizure account as the funding source for the initial unbudgeted appropriation.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Nine drones / 13 reported flights</h3>
        <p>Illinois' SFY 2024 drone report lists the Sheriff's Department as owning nine drones and reports 13 flights in the reporting data, including search, event-overwatch, damage-assessment, and crash-reconstruction uses.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">SECONDARY-SOURCE CONFIRMED</span>
        <h3>44 Flock readers reported in 2025</h3>
        <p>EFF's Atlas of Surveillance reports 44 Winnebago County Sheriff's Office Flock readers as of September 2025. The underlying records used for that count are not included in NoRec's published source set.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <details class="surveillance_sources" open>
      <summary>Sources</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://wincoil.gov/images/2024_05_23_Board_Agenda_PACKET.pdf">Winnebago County Board — May 23, 2024 agenda packet / Flock agreement</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://contracts.wincoil.gov/">Winnebago County contract register</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://agency.icjia-api.cloud/uploads/SFY_24_Drone_Report_with_Appendices_491dfa4810.pdf">Illinois SFY 2024 Freedom from Drone Surveillance Act report</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.atlasofsurveillance.org/search?agency=Winnebago+County+Sheriff%27s+Office">EFF Atlas of Surveillance — Winnebago County Sheriff's Office</a></li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">ROCKFORD POLICE DEPARTMENT</p>
    <h2>Flock, Axon, cameras, analytics, and gunshot detection</h2>
    <div class="finding_grid">
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>2026 Flock contract</h3>
        <p>Rockford approved a $443,998.16 sole-source Flock contract in 2026. The city's supporting memorandum says earlier retail-crime grants funded Flock Operating System software, Flock license-plate readers, and Flock PTZ cameras for the Police Department Intelligence Unit.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>150+ mobile and stationary LPRs</h3>
        <p>Rockford's crime-reduction page states the city purchased more than 150 mobile and stationary license-plate readers.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Axon body-worn cameras</h3>
        <p>Rockford's body-camera policy requires recordings to be uploaded through AXON docking stations and stored in a secure web-based digital-media system.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Six drones / eight reported flights</h3>
        <p>The SFY 2024 statewide drone report records six Rockford Police Department drones and eight flights during the reporting period.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Fixed cameras</h3>
        <p>The city reports that it quadrupled the number of cameras in high-traffic and high-crime areas.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Gunshot-detection software</h3>
        <p>Rockford lists gunshot-detection software among its enforcement technology. A 2022 City Council action also approved a Flock package containing gunshot detection and 64 license-plate readers.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Social-media analytics</h3>
        <p>The city states it instituted social-analytic software to track criminal use of social media. The current public page does not identify the vendor or product.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Flock renewal funding continues</h3>
        <p>A 2026 grant action states FY2027 organized-retail-crime funding will partially cover annual subscription fees for Flock Safety License Plate Reader Video Wing Enhanced.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

